{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018CNBSXLmWtx15ZPzqKLKft","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **methodology and experiments**. \n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Do NOT consult any external information — review only what is presented below.\n\n---\n\n## Paper: \"Circular Evaluation as Systematic Risk in Weak Supervision for Agent Progress Monitoring\"\n\n**Abstract:** Weak supervision pipelines for detecting progress stagnation in LLM agents face a subtle but critical evaluation pitfall: when both training labels and evaluation metrics derive from the same labeling functions (LFs), methods that reverse-engineer LF heuristics appear dominant while methods that learn genuine semantic signals are penalized. The authors construct a 650-window human-annotated benchmark (κ=0.76) from SWE-agent trajectories and reveal that ~22% of majority-vote labels are incorrect—predominantly missing semantically diverse stagnation where agents perform varied but ineffective actions. Structural feature classifiers, which achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels, collapse to 0.78–0.80 on gold labels (−19.5 to −21.5pp), while embedding-based probes drop only 8.6–10.1pp and surpass structural methods on gold (0.82–0.84 vs. 0.78–0.80). An overlap ablation isolating 12 LF-correlated features confirms that >55% of structural signal is circular (residual F1=0.70). Instance-isolated cross-agent evaluation reduces structural GB from 0.967 to 0.89 (−7.7pp), shifting the probe crossover point from 50% to ~8% fine-tuning data. LLM-as-judge with 5-shot chain-of-thought reaches 0.81 gold F1, matching probe and structural baselines. Results reframe the narrative from \"structural dominance\" to \"circular evaluation as systematic risk,\" contributing an annotated benchmark, a quantification methodology for circular bias, and evidence that embeddings learn beyond LF heuristics.\n\n**Section 1 – Introduction:** Introduces agent progress stagnation as a key operational challenge. Weak supervision with LFs (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation) and majority vote produces training labels. Prior work showed structural feature classifiers achieving >0.99 F1. This paper argues this \"dominance\" is a circular evaluation artifact. 5 key findings enumerated: (1) 22% MV label error, (2) structural collapse on gold, (3) overlap ablation >55% circular, (4) instance isolation drops structural GB from 0.967→0.89, (5) LLM 5-shot+CoT reaches 0.81.\n\n**Section 2 – Related Work:** Agent monitoring (cycle detectors CDCS+CDSA, SpecRA, SlopCodeBench, PRMs); Weak supervision for sequential data (data programming, i.i.d. violation); LLM self-evaluation (Reflexion, LATS, external judge).\n\n**Section 3 – Problem Setup:** Trajectories segmented into k=5 overlapping windows. Binary: progress vs. stagnation. 3 LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, OB. MV with conf≥2 for labels. Methods: Structural RF/GB (30 features), Embedding probes (GRU over Qwen3/E5/BGE), LLM-as-judge (Claude Sonnet 4, 0-shot and 5-shot+CoT), Temporal label models (TWV/HMM, negative finding in appendix).\n\n**Section 4 – Gold Benchmark:** N=650 from 15,401 SWE-agent windows (conf≥2). Stratified: 201 stag, 449 prog (30.9% stag rate). Two annotators, κ=0.76, 31% adjudication. Gold-MV agree on 507/650 (78%). Among 143 disagreements: 86 (60.1%) MV=prog→gold=stag (LFs miss semantic stagnation), 57 (39.9%) MV=stag→gold=prog (repetitive but productive).\n\n**Section 5 – Experiments:**\n\nTable 2 (Gold-Validated, N=650):\n| Method | LF F1 | Gold F1 | Δpp | Stag F1 | Prog F1 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural RF | .995 | .78 | −21.5 | .68 | .88 |\n| Structural GB | .995 | .80 | −19.5 | .71 | .89 |\n| Probe (Qwen) | .906 | .82 | −8.6 | .76 | .88 |\n| Probe (E5) | .936 | .83 | −10.6 | .77 | .89 |\n| Probe (BGE) | .941 | .84 | −10.1 | .78 | .90 |\n| LLM 0-shot | .663 | .73 | +6.8 | .66 | .80 |\n| LLM 5-shot+CoT | — | .81 | — | .75 | .87 |\n| TWV Labels | — | .72 | — | .63 | .81 |\n| TWV Probe | .952 | .80 | −15.2 | .73 | .87 |\n| MV Labels | 1.00 | .78 | — | — | — |\n\nTable 3 (Disagreement windows, N=143):\n| Method | Macro F1 | Follows Gold % | Follows MV % |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural RF | .36 | 35.7 | 64.3 |\n| Structural GB | .39 | 38.5 | 61.5 |\n| Probe (Qwen) | .53 | 52.4 | 47.6 |\n| Probe (BGE) | .56 | 55.2 | 44.8 |\n| LLM Judge 0-shot | .49 | 48.3 | 51.7 |\n\nTable 5 (Overlap ablation, N=650):\n| Feature Set | Nf | LF F1 | Gold F1 | LF–Gold Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Full structural | 30 | .995 | .80 | .195 |\n| Overlap-only | 12 | .98 | .77 | .21 |\n| Residual | 18 | .87 | .70 | .17 |\n| Probe Qwen | — | .906 | .82 | .086 |\n| Probe BGE | — | .941 | .84 | .101 |\n\nTable 6 (Top-5 residual features): action_diversity_entropy (Gini .18, corr w/ cmd_rep .61), trajectory_position_norm (.14, .08), mean_output_length (.12, .23), step_duration_variance (.09, .15), action_length_mean (.08, .31).\n\nTable 7 (Instance isolation, cross-agent):\n| Method | Original | Isolated | Δpp |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural GB | .967 | .89 | −7.7 |\n| Structural RF | .912 | .86 | −5.2 |\n| Probe 0-shot | .598 | .58 | −1.8 |\n| Probe 10% FT | .918 | .91 | −0.8 |\n| Probe 50% FT | .968 | .96 | −0.8 |\n\nTable 8 (Crossover analysis): Under isolation, probe 10% FT surpasses structural GB (0.91 vs. 0.89). Crossover shifts from ~50% to ~8% FT data.\n\n**Section 6 – Discussion:** Three-pronged evidence for circular evaluation risk. Probe gold-robustness (42% of FPs validated by gold). Method convergence at annotation ceiling (0.78–0.84, bounded by κ=0.76). Practical recommendations: zero-data→structural, ≥10% data→probe, always use gold evaluation.\n\n**Section 7 – Conclusion:** Summarizes findings.\n\n**Limitations:** Single agent (SWE-agent) for gold set; only two agents for cross-agent; κ=0.76 limits ceiling; temporal label model negative finding limited to 3-LF regime.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review context)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes these imagined experiments:\n1. **gold-set** (N=650, κ=0.76, all methods re-evaluated)\n2. **llm-judge-fewshot** (5-shot+CoT, Claude Sonnet 4, $15.20 cost)\n3. **structural-feature-overlap-ablation** (12 overlap vs. 18 residual features)\n4. **cross-agent-instance-isolation** (847 shared instances removed, 28.3% overlap)\n\nAll four experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n---\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review with these sections:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n### 6. Soundness Rating (1-5 scale: 1=fundamentally flawed, 5=rigorous and complete)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n### 8. Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Focus on methodological soundness, statistical rigor, experimental completeness, and whether the evidence supports the claims. Identify any gaps between claimed and demonstrated results."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29888,"cache_read_input_tokens":23498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29888},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"b6082aef-67aa-4995-b767-4d6dbf0b6bee"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a790209156b638b74","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **methodology and experiments**. \n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Do NOT consult any external information — review only what is presented below.\n\n---\n\n## Paper: \"Circular Evaluation as Systematic Risk in Weak Supervision for Agent Progress Monitoring\"\n\n**Abstract:** Weak supervision pipelines for detecting progress stagnation in LLM agents face a subtle but critical evaluation pitfall: when both training labels and evaluation metrics derive from the same labeling functions (LFs), methods that reverse-engineer LF heuristics appear dominant while methods that learn genuine semantic signals are penalized. The authors construct a 650-window human-annotated benchmark (κ=0.76) from SWE-agent trajectories and reveal that ~22% of majority-vote labels are incorrect—predominantly missing semantically diverse stagnation where agents perform varied but ineffective actions. Structural feature classifiers, which achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels, collapse to 0.78–0.80 on gold labels (−19.5 to −21.5pp), while embedding-based probes drop only 8.6–10.1pp and surpass structural methods on gold (0.82–0.84 vs. 0.78–0.80). An overlap ablation isolating 12 LF-correlated features confirms that >55% of structural signal is circular (residual F1=0.70). Instance-isolated cross-agent evaluation reduces structural GB from 0.967 to 0.89 (−7.7pp), shifting the probe crossover point from 50% to ~8% fine-tuning data. LLM-as-judge with 5-shot chain-of-thought reaches 0.81 gold F1, matching probe and structural baselines. Results reframe the narrative from \"structural dominance\" to \"circular evaluation as systematic risk,\" contributing an annotated benchmark, a quantification methodology for circular bias, and evidence that embeddings learn beyond LF heuristics.\n\n**Section 1 – Introduction:** Introduces agent progress stagnation as a key operational challenge. Weak supervision with LFs (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation) and majority vote produces training labels. Prior work showed structural feature classifiers achieving >0.99 F1. This paper argues this \"dominance\" is a circular evaluation artifact. 5 key findings enumerated: (1) 22% MV label error, (2) structural collapse on gold, (3) overlap ablation >55% circular, (4) instance isolation drops structural GB from 0.967→0.89, (5) LLM 5-shot+CoT reaches 0.81.\n\n**Section 2 – Related Work:** Agent monitoring (cycle detectors CDCS+CDSA, SpecRA, SlopCodeBench, PRMs); Weak supervision for sequential data (data programming, i.i.d. violation); LLM self-evaluation (Reflexion, LATS, external judge).\n\n**Section 3 – Problem Setup:** Trajectories segmented into k=5 overlapping windows. Binary: progress vs. stagnation. 3 LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, OB. MV with conf≥2 for labels. Methods: Structural RF/GB (30 features), Embedding probes (GRU over Qwen3/E5/BGE), LLM-as-judge (Claude Sonnet 4, 0-shot and 5-shot+CoT), Temporal label models (TWV/HMM, negative finding in appendix).\n\n**Section 4 – Gold Benchmark:** N=650 from 15,401 SWE-agent windows (conf≥2). Stratified: 201 stag, 449 prog (30.9% stag rate). Two annotators, κ=0.76, 31% adjudication. Gold-MV agree on 507/650 (78%). Among 143 disagreements: 86 (60.1%) MV=prog→gold=stag (LFs miss semantic stagnation), 57 (39.9%) MV=stag→gold=prog (repetitive but productive).\n\n**Section 5 – Experiments:**\n\nTable 2 (Gold-Validated, N=650):\n| Method | LF F1 | Gold F1 | Δpp | Stag F1 | Prog F1 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural RF | .995 | .78 | −21.5 | .68 | .88 |\n| Structural GB | .995 | .80 | −19.5 | .71 | .89 |\n| Probe (Qwen) | .906 | .82 | −8.6 | .76 | .88 |\n| Probe (E5) | .936 | .83 | −10.6 | .77 | .89 |\n| Probe (BGE) | .941 | .84 | −10.1 | .78 | .90 |\n| LLM 0-shot | .663 | .73 | +6.8 | .66 | .80 |\n| LLM 5-shot+CoT | — | .81 | — | .75 | .87 |\n| TWV Labels | — | .72 | — | .63 | .81 |\n| TWV Probe | .952 | .80 | −15.2 | .73 | .87 |\n| MV Labels | 1.00 | .78 | — | — | — |\n\nTable 3 (Disagreement windows, N=143):\n| Method | Macro F1 | Follows Gold % | Follows MV % |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural RF | .36 | 35.7 | 64.3 |\n| Structural GB | .39 | 38.5 | 61.5 |\n| Probe (Qwen) | .53 | 52.4 | 47.6 |\n| Probe (BGE) | .56 | 55.2 | 44.8 |\n| LLM Judge 0-shot | .49 | 48.3 | 51.7 |\n\nTable 5 (Overlap ablation, N=650):\n| Feature Set | Nf | LF F1 | Gold F1 | LF–Gold Gap |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Full structural | 30 | .995 | .80 | .195 |\n| Overlap-only | 12 | .98 | .77 | .21 |\n| Residual | 18 | .87 | .70 | .17 |\n| Probe Qwen | — | .906 | .82 | .086 |\n| Probe BGE | — | .941 | .84 | .101 |\n\nTable 6 (Top-5 residual features): action_diversity_entropy (Gini .18, corr w/ cmd_rep .61), trajectory_position_norm (.14, .08), mean_output_length (.12, .23), step_duration_variance (.09, .15), action_length_mean (.08, .31).\n\nTable 7 (Instance isolation, cross-agent):\n| Method | Original | Isolated | Δpp |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Structural GB | .967 | .89 | −7.7 |\n| Structural RF | .912 | .86 | −5.2 |\n| Probe 0-shot | .598 | .58 | −1.8 |\n| Probe 10% FT | .918 | .91 | −0.8 |\n| Probe 50% FT | .968 | .96 | −0.8 |\n\nTable 8 (Crossover analysis): Under isolation, probe 10% FT surpasses structural GB (0.91 vs. 0.89). Crossover shifts from ~50% to ~8% FT data.\n\n**Section 6 – Discussion:** Three-pronged evidence for circular evaluation risk. Probe gold-robustness (42% of FPs validated by gold). Method convergence at annotation ceiling (0.78–0.84, bounded by κ=0.76). Practical recommendations: zero-data→structural, ≥10% data→probe, always use gold evaluation.\n\n**Section 7 – Conclusion:** Summarizes findings.\n\n**Limitations:** Single agent (SWE-agent) for gold set; only two agents for cross-agent; κ=0.76 limits ceiling; temporal label model negative finding limited to 3-LF regime.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review context)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes these imagined experiments:\n1. **gold-set** (N=650, κ=0.76, all methods re-evaluated)\n2. **llm-judge-fewshot** (5-shot+CoT, Claude Sonnet 4, $15.20 cost)\n3. **structural-feature-overlap-ablation** (12 overlap vs. 18 residual features)\n4. **cross-agent-instance-isolation** (847 shared instances removed, 28.3% overlap)\n\nAll four experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n---\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review with these sections:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points, focus on methodology and experimental design)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Analyses\n### 6. Soundness Rating (1-5 scale: 1=fundamentally flawed, 5=rigorous and complete)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n### 8. Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Focus on methodological soundness, statistical rigor, experimental completeness, and whether the evidence supports the claims. Identify any gaps between claimed and demonstrated results.","uuid":"f6c475dc-51a6-40fa-8c91-c37141664da3","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db"}
