{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A5aeNdQq9pWgWVsseX3x6q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\tWe evaluate stagnation detection across four axes: in-domain accuracy, cross-agent transfer, robustness to design choices, and temporal dynamics of labeling functions.\n5\tAll reported metrics are evaluated against silver labels derived from majority vote over three labeling functions (\\S\\ref{sec:method}).\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Circular evaluation caveat.}\n8\tStructural features (command repetition ratios, file operation counts, edit distances) are computed from the same signals that the labeling functions use to produce silver labels.\n9\tConsequently, the in-domain structural F1 of 0.995 reflects an upper bound on how well these features can \\emph{reverse-engineer} the labeling functions, not detection accuracy against true ground truth.\n10\tThe cross-agent evaluation on OpenHands (0.967 macro-F1) is non-circular: models trained on SWE-Agent LF distributions are tested on a different agent whose LF firing patterns differ substantially (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n11\tA human-annotated gold set provides the definitive non-circular evaluation (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n12\t\n13\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n14\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n15\t\\label{sec:setup}\n16\t\n17\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n18\tWe extract sliding windows of $k{=}5$ consecutive actions from agent trajectories.\n19\t\\textbf{SWE-Agent} trajectories from SWE-bench \\citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent} yield 54,809 windows with a stagnation ratio of 28.5\\%.\n20\t\\textbf{OpenHands} (OH) trajectories \\citep{wang2025openhands} yield 150,652 windows with a stagnation ratio of 20.9\\%.\n21\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote over three labeling functions; confidence equals the number of agreeing LFs (1--3).\n22\tUnless stated otherwise, evaluation uses the conf${\\geq}$2 subset: $N{=}1{,}824$ for SWE-Agent (485 stagnation, 1,339 progress) and $N{=}43{,}376$ for OH.\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{table}[t]\n25\t\\centering\n26\t\\small\n27\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrc}\n28\t\\toprule\n29\t\\textbf{Agent} & \\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{Windows} & \\textbf{Stag\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Conf${\\geq}$2} \\\\\n30\t\\midrule\n31\tSWE-Agent & SWE-bench & 54,809 & 28.5 & 15,401 \\\\\n32\tOpenHands & CodeAct   & 150,652 & 20.9 & 43,376 \\\\\n33\t\\bottomrule\n34\t\\end{tabular}\n35\t\\caption{Dataset statistics. Stagnation ratios differ substantially across agents, reflecting different action distributions.}\n36\t\\label{tab:dataset_stats}\n37\t\\end{table}\n38\t\n39\t\\paragraph{Detection methods.}\n40\t(1)~\\textbf{Structural features}: 30 hand-crafted features (repetition rates, file operation counts, edit distances, output entropy) fed to Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n41\t(2)~\\textbf{ProgressProbe}: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} embeddings (1024-dim) processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with attention \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} (hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1), early-stopped at epoch~3 with patience~5.\n42\t(3)~\\textbf{Mean-pool + LR}: Mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression.\n43\t(4)~\\textbf{LLM-as-Judge}: Claude Sonnet 4.6 zero-shot window-level classification (\\$11.66 API cost for the SWE test set).\n44\t(5)~\\textbf{Cosine similarity}: Cosine distance between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n45\t\n46\t\\paragraph{Metrics.}\n47\tMacro-F1 is the primary metric. We also report per-class F1 for stagnation (F1\\textsubscript{stag}) and progress (F1\\textsubscript{prog}).\n48\t\n49\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n50\t\\subsection{Main Results}\n51\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n52\t\n53\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} presents in-domain results on the SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set and cross-agent transfer to OpenHands.\n54\t\n55\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n56\t\\centering\n57\t\\small\n58\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc cc}\n59\t\\toprule\n60\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{SWE-Agent In-Domain} (conf${\\geq}$2)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{OpenHands Transfer}} \\\\\n61\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6}\n62\t\\textbf{Method} & Macro-F1 & F1\\textsubscript{stag} & F1\\textsubscript{prog} & All & Conf${\\geq}$2 \\\\\n63\t\\midrule\n64\tStructural RF$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & 0.9123 & \\underline{0.9959} \\\\\n65\tStructural GB$^\\dagger$       & \\textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & \\textbf{0.9674} & \\textbf{0.9918} \\\\\n66\tStructural LR$^\\dagger$       & 0.9874 & 0.9815 & 0.9933 & 0.7801 & 0.9671 \\\\\n67\tProgressProbe (GRU)           & \\underline{0.9055} & 0.8593 & 0.9518 & 0.598$^a$ & 0.598$^a$ \\\\\n68\t\\quad + full OH fine-tune      & ---    & ---    & ---    & 0.976$^b$ & 0.976$^b$ \\\\\n69\tMean-pool + LR                & 0.8435 & 0.7620 & 0.9250 & ---    & --- \\\\\n70\tLLM-as-Judge                  & 0.6625 & 0.6055 & 0.7195 & ---    & --- \\\\\n71\tCosine Similarity             & 0.5606 & ---    & ---    & ---    & --- \\\\\n72\t\\bottomrule\n73\t\\end{tabular}\n74\t\\caption{Stagnation detection results. In-domain evaluation on SWE-Agent conf${\\geq}$2 test set ($N{=}1{,}824$) and zero-shot cross-agent transfer to OpenHands. $^\\dagger$Structural metrics are evaluated against LF-derived labels and reflect LF-reconstructibility rather than ground-truth accuracy (see circular evaluation caveat). $^a$Probe zero-shot macro-F1 is identical on all-windows and conf${\\geq}$2 subsets (0.598 to three decimal places). $^b$Probe fine-tuned macro-F1 confirmed on conf${\\geq}$2 via paired bootstrap with GB ($p{<}0.001$); all-windows value from the same model. Best in \\textbf{bold}, second-best \\underline{underlined}.}\n75\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n76\t\\end{table*}\n77\t\n78\tIn-domain, structural RF achieves 0.995 macro-F1, exceeding the GRU probe by 8.96\\,pp.\n79\tHowever, the structural--probe gap is inflated by circular evaluation: structural features reconstruct the LFs that generated the labels.\n80\tThe more informative comparison is among non-circular methods: the GRU probe outperforms mean-pool~+~LR by 6.2\\,pp, confirming that temporal modeling within windows adds value beyond static embedding aggregation.\n81\tThe zero-shot LLM judge (0.663) underperforms all learned methods despite substantially higher cost (\\$11.66 per test set).\n82\t\n83\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n84\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}\n85\t\\label{sec:cross_agent}\n86\t\n87\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} (right columns) shows models trained on SWE-Agent and evaluated on OpenHands without any OH training data.\n88\tStructural GB transfers best, achieving 0.967 macro-F1 on all OH windows.\n89\tRF drops to 0.912 on all-confidence OH data despite matching GB in-domain, suggesting GB's boosted ensemble is more robust to distributional shift.\n90\tBoth structural models exceed 0.99 macro-F1 on conf${\\geq}$2 OH windows (GB 0.992, RF 0.996), where label noise is lower.\n91\t\n92\tThe probe's zero-shot transfer (0.598) lags structural GB by 36.9\\,pp, but full OH fine-tuning closes this gap dramatically: the fine-tuned probe reaches 0.976 macro-F1 on all OH windows, surpassing structural GB (0.967) by 0.9\\,pp (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n93\tThe asymmetry between structural and learned methods is thus stage-dependent: in the zero-data regime, structural features transfer naturally while learned embeddings do not (+36.9\\,pp gap); with target-domain fine-tuning, the probe surpasses structural features (+0.9\\,pp on all windows).\n94\tThe practical implication is a deployment trade-off: use structural features when no labeled data is available; invest in probe fine-tuning when annotation budget permits.\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Multi-seed stability.}\n97\tStructural RF: $0.995{\\pm}0.000$ in-domain (3 seeds, zero variance); GB cross-agent: $0.968{\\pm}0.002$.\n98\tThe probe shows higher variance: $0.919{\\pm}0.011$, though all seeds exceed 0.90.\n99\t\n100\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n101\t\\subsection{Robustness Analysis}\n102\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n103\t\n104\t\\paragraph{Encoder ablation.}\n105\tReplacing Qwen3-0.6B with BGE-M3 \\citep{chen2024bgem3} or E5-large-v2 \\citep{wang2024e5} yields probe macro-F1 of 0.941 and 0.936, respectively (vs.\\ 0.906 for Qwen3).\n106\tAll encoders exceed 0.90, indicating the probe's temporal architecture, not a specific encoder, drives performance. Full results appear in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:encoder_ablation}.\n107\t\n108\t\\paragraph{Window size.}\n109\t$k{=}5$ is optimal (0.931 macro-F1); $k{=}3$ drops 5.9\\,pp and $k{=}7$ drops 1.8\\,pp.\n110\tShorter windows lack sufficient context; longer windows dilute signal by spanning both progress and stagnation phases.\n111\tThe 0.931 figure differs from the main table's 0.906 because the window ablation uses a separate train/val/test split (seed~42, Qwen3-0.6B encoder) to enable controlled comparison across window sizes; cross-split variance accounts for the gap.\n112\tDetails in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:window_ablation}.\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Data scaling.}\n115\tFigure~\\ref{fig:data_scaling} compares data efficiency across methods.\n116\tStructural classifiers are extremely data-efficient: RF achieves 0.991 and GB 0.987 at just 10\\% training data, fully saturating by 50\\%.\n117\tThe probe scales monotonically from 0.805 (10\\%) to 0.906 (100\\%) but has not plateaued---a 10.1\\,pp drop that is roughly 20$\\times$ the structural degradation (${\\sim}$0.4--0.8\\,pp).\n118\tThis data efficiency further supports the structural signal thesis: the features capture patterns so regular that minimal training data suffices.\n119\t\n120\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n121\t\\centering\n122\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_data_scaling.pdf}\n123\t\\caption{Macro-F1 vs.\\ training data fraction. Structural classifiers (RF, GB) saturate by 50\\% data; the probe scales monotonically but remains 9\\,pp below at 100\\%.}\n124\t\\label{fig:data_scaling}\n125\t\\end{figure}\n126\t\n127\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n128\t\\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}\n129\t\\label{sec:lf_temporal}\n130\t\n131\tWe analyze when each LF first fires relative to the onset of stagnation episodes (Table~\\ref{tab:lf_temporal}, Figure~\\ref{fig:lf_temporal}).\n132\t\n133\t\\begin{table}[t]\n134\t\\centering\n135\t\\small\n136\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n137\t\\toprule\n138\t\\textbf{LF} & \\textbf{Lag} & \\textbf{Lead\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Conc.\\,\\%} & \\textbf{Lag\\,\\%} \\\\\n139\t\\midrule\n140\tcmd\\_rep      & $-1.87$ & 36.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n141\tfile\\_diff    & $-1.20$ & 17.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n142\toutcome\\_bp   & $+6.70$ & ---  & --- & 48.4 \\\\\n143\t\\bottomrule\n144\t\\end{tabular}\n145\t\\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions on SWE-Agent. Lag = mean steps relative to stagnation onset (negative = leading). Kruskal-Wallis $H{=}274.3$, $p{=}2.74{\\times}10^{-60}$; all pairwise comparisons significant after Bonferroni correction.}\n146\t\\label{tab:lf_temporal}\n147\t\\end{table}\n148\t\n149\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches.\n150\tCommand repetition leads stagnation onset by 1.87 steps on average, making it an early warning signal.\n151\tFile diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$ steps).\n152\tOutcome backpropagation lags by 6.70 steps, acting as a retrospective confirmation.\n153\t\n154\tAll pairwise differences are significant (Kruskal-Wallis $H{=}274.3$, $p{=}2.74{\\times}10^{-60}$; Bonferroni-corrected pairwise: cmd\\_rep vs.\\ file\\_diff $p{=}1.44{\\times}10^{-26}$, cmd\\_rep vs.\\ outcome\\_bp $p{=}6.67{\\times}10^{-39}$, file\\_diff vs.\\ outcome\\_bp $p{=}3.47{\\times}10^{-47}$).\n155\t\n156\t\\paragraph{Cross-agent reversal.}\n157\tOn OpenHands, cmd\\_rep shifts from leading ($-1.87$) to lagging ($+3.32$ steps).\n158\tUnder corrected OH labels (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}), cmd\\_rep fires on only 5.4\\% of OH windows with 97.9\\% precision---a high-precision, low-recall detector whose temporal role differs fundamentally from its SWE-Agent behavior.\n159\t\n160\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n161\t\\centering\n162\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_lf_temporal.pdf}\n163\t\\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions relative to stagnation onset on SWE-Agent. cmd\\_rep leads ($-1.87$ steps), file\\_diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$), outcome\\_bp lags ($+6.70$). Inset: OH reversal where cmd\\_rep becomes lagging.}\n164\t\\label{fig:lf_temporal}\n165\t\\end{figure}\n166\t\n167\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n168\t\\subsection{Label Quality and cmd\\_rep Coverage}\n169\t\\label{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}\n170\t\n171\tA label quality audit revealed that the OH embedding vectors used for silver labeling contained a version mismatch, causing systematic label errors.\n172\tAfter correction, the OH stagnation rate on the embedding subset (143,057 windows; short trajectories excluded) shifted from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\%, with 21,898 window labels flipping.\n173\tThis section quantifies how label quality and cmd\\_rep coverage jointly affect probe transfer.\n174\t\n175\t\\paragraph{Label correction impact on probe transfer.}\n176\tWith corrected labels, the 3-LF probe improves from zero-shot 0.598 to fine-tuned 0.976 (previously 0.751 under corrupted labels).\n177\tThe improvement is most dramatic at low data fractions: 5\\% fine-tuning reaches 0.850, exceeding the old 50\\% result (0.69) by 16.0\\,pp.\n178\tThe probe converges rapidly: at 50\\% OH data it reaches 0.968, essentially matching structural GB (0.967) and demonstrating that the structural advantage is specific to zero-data deployment (full curve in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:cross_agent_cmdrep}).\n179\tLabel quality, not model capacity, was the transfer bottleneck.\n180\t\n181\t\\paragraph{Prevalence-matched ablation.}\n182\tTo isolate cmd\\_rep's contribution, we compare 3-LF and 2-LF (no cmd\\_rep) models on the same test set with matched class prevalence.\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table}[t]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\small\n187\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n188\t\\toprule\n189\t\\textbf{LF Config} & \\textbf{Macro-F1} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{stag}} & \\textbf{F1\\textsubscript{prog}} \\\\\n190\t\\midrule\n191\t3-LF (w/ cmd\\_rep) & \\textbf{0.9763} & 0.960 & 0.993 \\\\\n192\t2-LF (no cmd\\_rep) & 0.6822 & 0.481 & 0.884 \\\\\n193\t\\midrule\n194\t$\\Delta$ & $-29.4$\\,pp & $-47.9$\\,pp & $-10.9$\\,pp \\\\\n195\t\\bottomrule\n196\t\\end{tabular}\n197\t\\caption{Prevalence-matched ablation on the same OH test set ($N{=}6{,}122$, stag rate 19.3\\%). Removing cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (McNemar $\\chi^2{=}1551$, $p{\\approx}0$).}\n198\t\\label{tab:cmd_rep_ablation}\n199\t\\end{table}\n200\t\n201\tRemoving cmd\\_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4\\,pp (McNemar $\\chi^2{=}1551$, $p{\\approx}0$), a statistically significant degradation driven primarily by stagnation recall ($-47.9$\\,pp F1\\textsubscript{stag}).\n202\t\n203\t\\paragraph{Coverage, not label quality, explains the gap.}\n204\tThe 29.4\\,pp drop is not caused by cmd\\_rep improving label accuracy.\n205\tAfter correction, cmd\\_rep fires on only 5.39\\% of OH windows (down from the previously reported 92.2\\% under corrupted embeddings) with 97.91\\% precision---a high-precision, low-recall signal.\n206\tOn the 4,070 windows where both 2-LF and 3-LF labels are defined, the two configurations agree on 100\\% of labels: cmd\\_rep does not change any label where the other two LFs already vote.\n207\tIts contribution is purely \\emph{coverage}: including cmd\\_rep expands the training set from ${\\sim}$4K to ${\\sim}$43K windows (a ${\\sim}$10$\\times$ increase).\n208\tThe 2-LF model's low F1 reflects data volume collapse---too few training examples to learn the stagnation distribution---rather than label corruption.\n209\t\n210\t\\paragraph{Implications for weak supervision.}\n211\tThese results reframe how labeling functions contribute to weak supervision pipelines.\n212\tA high-precision, low-recall LF like cmd\\_rep may appear redundant when its labels are a subset of the majority vote, yet removing it causes training data to collapse by an order of magnitude.\n213\tIn prevalence-matched evaluation, this data reduction manifests as a 29.4\\,pp F1 drop.\n214\tThe core contribution of such LFs is coverage provision, not label refinement: they extend the labelable portion of the input space rather than correcting other LFs' errors.\n215\t\n216\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n217\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n218\t\\label{sec:temporal_label_models}\n219\t\n220\tWe investigate whether any label aggregation method---temporal or generative---improves over majority vote (MV).\n221\tTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_models} compares five label models: three temporal (TWV, Lag-HMM, HMM) \\citep{ratner2017snorkel,safranchik2020linkedhmm} and two generative i.i.d.\\ models (FlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, Snorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal}).\n222\t\n223\t\\begin{table}[t]\n224\t\\centering\n225\t\\small\n226\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n227\t\\toprule\n228\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{MV Agr.} & \\textbf{Conf${\\geq}$2 F1} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs MV} & \\textbf{Probe F1$^\\ddagger$} \\\\\n229\t\\midrule\n230\tMV (baseline) & 1.000 & 0.895$^\\ast$ & --- & 0.847 \\\\\n231\t\\midrule\n232\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{Temporal}} \\\\\n233\tTWV ($\\sigma{=}2$) & 0.885 & \\textbf{0.900} & $+$0.6\\,pp & 0.821 \\\\\n234\tLag-HMM & 0.820 & 0.894 & $-$0.1\\,pp & --- \\\\\n235\tHMM & 0.685 & 0.750 & $-$14.4\\,pp & 0.656 \\\\\n236\t\\midrule\n237\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\emph{Generative (i.i.d.)}} \\\\\n238\tFlyingSquid & 0.820 & 0.782 & $-$11.3\\,pp & --- \\\\\n239\tSnorkel MeTaL & 0.423 & 0.258 & $-$63.6\\,pp & --- \\\\\n240\t\\bottomrule\n241\t\\end{tabular}\n242\t\\caption{Label model comparison. $^\\ast$MV conf${\\geq}$2 F1 is a self-evaluation baseline. $^\\ddagger$Downstream probe trained on each method's labels, evaluated on the full SWE-Agent test set ($N{=}8{,}101$) against MV labels. ``---'' = not evaluated downstream. No model consistently improves over MV.}\n243\t\\label{tab:temporal_models}\n244\t\\end{table}\n245\t\n246\t\\paragraph{Temporal models.}\n247\tTWV achieves the highest conf${\\geq}$2 F1 (0.900, $+$0.6\\,pp over MV), but this gain does not propagate downstream: a probe trained on TWV labels scores 0.821 vs.\\ 0.847 for MV-trained ($-$2.57\\,pp).\n248\tTWV flips 14.1\\% of conf${=}$1 labels, introducing noise that hurts probe training.\n249\tThe Gaussian kernel bandwidth $\\sigma$ is critical: $\\sigma{=}2$ is optimal; $\\sigma{=}1$ drops to 0.811 and $\\sigma{=}5$ to 0.808, making TWV impractical as a drop-in MV replacement.\n250\tHMM overpredicts stagnation (29,176 vs.\\ 25,633 progress windows), degrading conf${\\geq}$2 F1 by 14.4\\,pp and downstream performance to 0.656.\n251\tLag-HMM closely tracks MV ($-$0.1\\,pp) despite explicitly modeling temporal offsets.\n252\t\n253\t\\paragraph{Generative (i.i.d.) models.}\n254\tFlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, a closed-form triplet-based model, drops 11.3\\,pp below MV.\n255\tOn the full dataset, agreement with MV is 82\\%; all divergent labels flip progress$\\to$stagnation, introducing systematic stagnation bias.\n256\tSnorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal} fails catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp), with only 42.3\\% MV agreement.\n257\tBoth models assume conditional independence among LFs---an assumption violated by the shared behavioral signals underlying our three LFs---and lack the sequential structure to recover from this misspecification.\n258\t\n259\t\\paragraph{Root cause: insufficient inter-LF conflict.}\n260\tThese results constitute a negative finding: five label models spanning temporal aggregation and generative modeling all fail to improve over majority vote.\n261\tThree binary LFs produce at most $2^3{=}8$ voting patterns; conf${\\geq}$2 windows dominate the training set, leaving insufficient ambiguity for any aggregation method to exploit.\n262\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation sophistication---scaling to ${>}$10 LFs with higher disagreement rates is needed before advanced label models can add value.\n263\t\n264\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n265\t\\subsection{Gold Set Validation}\n266\t\\label{sec:gold_set}\n267\t\\goldset{Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending. Results will be added upon completion (expected 2026-05-17). This provides the non-circular ground truth evaluation for all methods.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"bd1880c4-1f11-45ff-81a3-1a085f50ca4c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:35:40.913Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MLeLtz98WYjFbEWkgUW2kY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01THWd3xiJoaJ8QvY3SpDrmv","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3585,"cache_read_input_tokens":17111,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3585,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":71,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"504c0758-6d01-4ae8-91ad-8c64554d959a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01THWd3xiJoaJ8QvY3SpDrmv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tLLM agents are increasingly deployed for complex, long-horizon software engineering tasks---resolving GitHub issues, debugging production systems, and iterating on multi-file codebases across dozens of editing rounds \\citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent,wang2025openhands}.\n5\tAs these deployments scale, a critical failure mode has emerged that is qualitatively different from crashes or syntax errors: the agent \\emph{stagnates}---continuing to produce actions that appear purposeful while making no meaningful progress toward the task goal.\n6\t\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document this degradation on SlopCodeBench, recording verbosity increases of 77\\% and quality drops of 75.5\\% in long-horizon coding trajectories, with no agent fully solving any benchmark problem end-to-end.\n7\tCrucially, the dominant failure pattern is not simple repetition but what we term \\emph{semantic stagnation}: the agent issues varied commands, edits different files, and generates plausible-looking outputs, yet its behavior is functionally stuck.\n8\tThis ``looks busy but going in circles'' pattern defeats existing monitoring tools, because surface-level action diversity masks the underlying lack of progress.\n9\tWe observe this phenomenon at scale: across trajectories from two architecturally distinct agents---SWE-Agent \\citep{yang2024sweagent} and OpenHands \\citep{wang2025openhands}---on SWE-bench tasks \\citep{jimenez2024swebench}, 28.5\\% and 20.9\\% of sliding windows exhibit stagnation under our silver labeling scheme, respectively, confirming that the failure mode is both prevalent and highly agent-dependent.\n10\t\n11\tExisting approaches address aspects of this problem but fall short in three ways.\n12\tFirst, \\emph{repetition detectors} such as cycle detection \\citep{george2025cycledetection} and SpecRA \\citep{pipis2025reasoningloop} identify structural repetition---exact or near-exact loops in action sequences---but by design miss semantically diverse stagnation where the agent varies its surface behavior while remaining functionally stuck.\n13\tSecond, \\emph{step-level scoring} via process reward models \\citep{xi2025agentprm} evaluates individual actions in isolation, discarding the window-level temporal dynamics that distinguish transient setbacks from sustained stagnation---a distinction that requires reasoning over sequences of consecutive actions \\citep{chen2025monitoring}.\n14\tA sequence of failed commands followed by a strategy switch, for instance, carries different diagnostic information than the same commands evaluated independently.\n15\tThird, \\emph{LLM self-evaluation} \\citep{shinn2023reflexion} is both expensive and fundamentally limited by the stuck model's own judgment; our LLM-as-judge baseline achieves only 0.663 macro-F1 at \\$11.66 per test set, well below lightweight feature-based alternatives.\n16\tMore fundamentally, none of these approaches asks the prior question: \\emph{what signals actually carry stagnation information?}\n17\tNo systematic study has investigated whether detecting subtle agent failure requires sophisticated semantic understanding or whether simpler structural patterns suffice.\n18\t\n19\tWe address this question through a systematic empirical study (Figure~\\ref{fig:pipeline}).\n20\tUsing a weak supervision framework \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel}, we construct silver-labeled datasets from over 205K windows spanning SWE-Agent (54,809 windows, 28.5\\% silver-labeled stagnation) and OpenHands (150,652 windows, 20.9\\% stagnation).\n21\tThree labeling functions provide complementary noisy votes: command repetition ($\\lambda_1$) targets repetitive behavior, file diff delta ($\\lambda_2$) targets output productivity, and outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$) propagates task-level outcomes backward through the trajectory.\n22\tMajority vote aggregation produces window-level binary labels with confidence scores.\n23\tOur window-level formulation ($k{=}5$ consecutive actions, stride 1) captures short-range temporal patterns---such as repeated failures followed by strategy changes---that step-level approaches cannot express, while providing the granularity to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a trajectory, enabling targeted interventions.\n24\tAgainst these silver labels, we systematically compare hand-crafted structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.\n25\t\n26\tOur study yields three principal findings.\n27\t\n28\t\\textbf{Finding 1: Structural features dominate zero-shot cross-agent transfer.}\n29\tThirty hand-crafted features---command repetition rates, file operation counts, edit distances---achieve 0.995 macro-F1 in-domain (Random Forest) and 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1 (Gradient Boosting, SWE-Agent $\\to$ OpenHands) with zero target data, outperforming the GRU embedding probe \\citep{cho2014gru} zero-shot by 36.9\\,pp (0.598 on OpenHands, near chance) and the zero-shot LLM-as-judge baseline (0.663) by 30.4\\,pp.\n30\tHowever, with 100\\% target fine-tuning the probe reaches 0.976 macro-F1, surpassing the structural baseline (0.967)---demonstrating that probe representations recover cross-agent generalization with sufficient adaptation data.\n31\tWe note that in-domain evaluation uses silver labels (\\S\\ref{sec:circular}); cross-agent transfer and a pending gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}) provide non-circular validation.\n32\tThe structural advantage is encoder-agnostic: three embedding backbones \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} all yield probe F1 above 0.90, confirming that the probe's temporal architecture rather than a specific pretrained encoder drives performance.\n33\tThe in-domain score of 0.995 reflects circular evaluation---structural features overlap with LF inputs---but the cross-agent score of 0.967, where models trained on one agent's LF distribution are tested on another with substantially different firing patterns, breaks this circularity.\n34\tA feature overlap ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap}) confirms that removing all 11 LF-overlapping features retains 0.952 in-domain F1 but collapses cross-agent F1 to 0.566: the features that generalize are precisely those encoding universal stagnation signals.\n35\tThis finding challenges the prevailing intuition that detecting subtle agent failures requires complex language understanding, and suggests that lightweight structural monitoring---no GPU, under 5\\,ms per window---may be more practical than neural or LLM-based approaches for real-time agent oversight.\n36\t\n37\t\\textbf{Finding 2: Label quality, not LF selection, is the dominant cross-agent transfer bottleneck.}\n38\tLabeling functions exhibit systematic temporal dynamics that reverse across agents: command repetition ($\\lambda_1$) leads stagnation onset on SWE-Agent by $-1.87$ steps but lags on OpenHands by $+3.32$ steps.\n39\tA label quality audit reveals that fixing OH label version bias---correcting stagnation rate from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\%---improves 5\\% fine-tuning F1 from 0.570 (under previous label version) to 0.850.\n40\tPrevalence-matched ablation shows that $\\lambda_1$'s core value is coverage ($-29.4$\\,pp when removed, $p \\approx 0$), not label precision: it fires on only 5.39\\% of OH windows with precision 97.91\\% (FP rate 2.3\\%).\n41\t\n42\t\\textbf{Finding 3: No label model improves over majority vote.}\n43\tFive label models---three temporal (TWV, HMM \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, lag-aware HMM) and two generative (FlyingSquid \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, Snorkel MeTaL \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal})---all fail to surpass majority vote on downstream detection.\n44\tTWV achieves marginally higher self-evaluated F1 but degrades downstream probe performance by 2.57\\,pp; Snorkel MeTaL collapses catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp).\n45\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation sophistication: three binary LFs produce too little inter-LF conflict for any method to exploit.\n46\t\n47\tOur contributions are:\n48\t\\begin{enumerate}\n49\t    \\item \\textbf{Structural signal discovery.} We demonstrate that 30 lightweight structural features achieve near-perfect stagnation detection (0.995 in-domain, 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1) with zero target data, outperforming the probe zero-shot by 36.9\\,pp and the LLM-as-judge by 30.4\\,pp, with no GPU and under 5\\,ms per window. With full fine-tuning the probe reaches within 1\\,pp of the structural baseline (0.976 vs.\\ 0.967 macro-F1), delineating when structural monitoring suffices and when fine-tuning is warranted.\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Label quality as transfer bottleneck.} We show that labeling functions exhibit agent-specific temporal reversals and that fixing label version bias (stag rate 6.56\\%$\\to$21.4\\%) dominates LF selection for cross-agent transfer, informing future weak-supervision design on sequential agent data.\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{Label model negative finding.} We show that five label models---three temporal and two generative---fail to improve over majority vote for downstream detection, despite clear temporal structure in LF firing patterns. The bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation method: the 3-LF regime produces insufficient inter-LF conflict for any approach to add value.\n52\t\\end{enumerate}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"7c16eb01-aa9d-4df2-af8b-287ed61e4fe4","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:35:41.366Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MLeLtz98WYjFbEWkgUW2kY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0199hK565VkDmHeevMz1ykDL","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/method.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3585,"cache_read_input_tokens":17111,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3585,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":71,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"7d17ccb2-8dc9-41b3-8e4f-39724761129b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0199hK565VkDmHeevMz1ykDL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tWe frame stagnation detection as window-level binary classification over agent trajectories.\n5\tSilver labels are generated via weak supervision from three labeling functions, then used to train and evaluate three detection approaches: structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.\n6\tFigure~\\ref{fig:pipeline} provides a system overview.\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{figure*}[t]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_pipeline.pdf}\n11\t\\caption{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}\n12\t\\label{fig:pipeline}\n13\t\\end{figure*}\n14\t\n15\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n16\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n17\t\\label{sec:problem}\n18\t\n19\tGiven an agent trajectory $T = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (e.g., a shell command, file edit, or API call), we partition it into overlapping sliding windows $w_i = (a_i, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ with window size $k{=}5$ and stride 1.\n20\tThe task is to assign each window a binary label $y_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}$, where 0 denotes \\emph{stagnation} (the agent is stuck or unproductive) and 1 denotes \\emph{progress} (the agent is making meaningful headway toward the task goal).\n21\t\n22\tThe window formulation offers two advantages over alternatives.\n23\tCompared to step-level scoring, windows capture short-range temporal patterns---such as a sequence of repeated failed attempts---that individual actions cannot express.\n24\tCompared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.\n25\t\n26\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n27\t\\subsection{Weak Supervision Pipeline}\n28\t\\label{sec:weak_supervision}\n29\t\n30\tNo gold-standard annotations exist for agent stagnation at the window level.\n31\tManual annotation is expensive: each window requires reviewing $k$ actions with full context (command text, outputs, file diffs), taking an estimated 2--3 minutes per window.\n32\tWe therefore adopt a data programming approach \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel}: multiple labeling functions (LFs) provide noisy votes that are aggregated into silver labels.\n33\tEach LF $\\lambda_j : w_i \\to \\{0, 1, \\bot\\}$ examines a window and emits a vote: stagnation (0), progress (1), or abstain ($\\bot$).\n34\t\n35\t\\subsubsection{Labeling Functions}\n36\t\\label{sec:labeling_functions}\n37\t\n38\tWe design three LFs that target complementary signals of stagnation (Table~\\ref{tab:lf_design}).\n39\t\n40\t\\paragraph{Command repetition ($\\lambda_1$).}\n41\tComputes the proportion of repeated commands within window $w_i$.\n42\tA high repetition ratio triggers a stagnation vote, capturing overt repetitive behavior such as re-running failing commands.\n43\tThis LF never abstains: it votes stagnation when the repetition ratio exceeds a threshold and progress otherwise.\n44\tIt votes stagnation on 65.5\\% of SWE-Agent windows but only 5.4\\% of OpenHands windows; the lower OH rate reflects OH's greater action diversity, which reduces command repetition.\n45\tOn OH, cmd\\_rep achieves 97.9\\% precision with 26.0\\% recall, and shifts from leading stagnation onset ($-1.87$ steps on SWE-Agent) to lagging ($+3.32$ steps), demonstrating agent-specific temporal dynamics (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{File diff delta ($\\lambda_2$).}\n48\tMeasures the fraction of steps within $w_i$ that produce code modifications (file creates, edits, or deletes).\n49\tA low modification frequency triggers a stagnation vote; windows with more than one edited file receive a progress vote; windows with exactly one or zero edits abstain ($\\bot$).\n50\tOn OpenHands, this LF achieves 87.5\\% recall on stagnation windows (precision 99.9\\%), making it the most reliable cross-agent signal.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$).}\n53\tUses the final task outcome (resolved/unresolved) as distant supervision.\n54\tFor unresolved tasks, the last two windows and any windows following a timeout or stuck signal receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes in the corresponding windows.\n55\tAll other windows abstain ($\\bot$).\n56\tUnlike $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, which operate on local window content, $\\lambda_3$ propagates a global signal backward through time.\n57\tThis makes it a form of distant supervision: individually noisy, but complementary to the real-time signals when aggregated.\n58\t\n59\t\\begin{table}[t]\n60\t\\centering\n61\t\\small\n62\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n63\t\\toprule\n64\t\\textbf{LF} & \\textbf{Signal} & \\textbf{SWE Stag\\,\\%} & \\textbf{OH Stag\\,\\%} \\\\\n65\t\\midrule\n66\t$\\lambda_1$ (cmd\\_rep)    & Repetition & 65.5 & 5.4 \\\\\n67\t$\\lambda_2$ (file\\_diff)  & Output     & 57.3 & 17.8 \\\\\n68\t$\\lambda_3$ (outcome\\_bp) & Outcome    & 23.9 & 16.8 \\\\\n69\t\\bottomrule\n70\t\\end{tabular}\n71\t\\caption{Labeling function design. Stag\\,\\% = fire rate, i.e., fraction of windows on which the LF votes stagnation (remaining windows receive progress or abstain). SWE = SWE-Agent; OH = OpenHands. OH values reflect corrected action embeddings (\\S\\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).}\n72\t\\label{tab:lf_design}\n73\t\\end{table}\n74\t\n75\t\\subsubsection{Label Aggregation}\n76\t\\label{sec:label_aggregation}\n77\t\n78\tSilver labels are produced by majority vote among non-abstaining LFs:\n79\t\\begin{equation}\n80\ty_i = \\text{MV}\\bigl(\\{\\lambda_j(w_i) : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot\\}\\bigr)\n81\t\\label{eq:mv}\n82\t\\end{equation}\n83\tWe define the confidence of each label as the number of non-abstaining LFs that agree with the winning label: $\\text{conf}_i = |\\{j : \\lambda_j(w_i) \\neq \\bot \\;\\text{and}\\; \\lambda_j(w_i) = y_i\\}| \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$.\n84\tConf${=}1$ arises when a single non-abstaining LF determines the label---either because only one LF votes or because two LFs disagree with no clear majority---producing the most ambiguous labels.\n85\tThe conf${\\geq}$2 subset, where at least two non-abstaining LFs agree on the winning label, serves as the primary evaluation set throughout this work.\n86\tThis stratification allows us to separately analyze model behavior on high-confidence vs.\\ ambiguous windows (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}).\n87\t\n88\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n89\t\\subsection{Detection Methods}\n90\t\\label{sec:detection_methods}\n91\t\n92\t\\subsubsection{Structural Features}\n93\t\\label{sec:structural_features}\n94\t\n95\tWe extract a 30-dimensional feature vector from each window, requiring no pretrained model or GPU.\n96\tFeatures span four categories:\n97\t(1)~\\emph{repetition}: exact command repeat ratio, fuzzy match ratio (Levenshtein-based), and unique command ratio;\n98\t(2)~\\emph{file operations}: counts of file creates, modifications, and deletions within the window;\n99\t(3)~\\emph{edit patterns}: edit distances between consecutive actions, capturing how much the agent's behavior changes step-to-step;\n100\t(4)~\\emph{output statistics}: entropy of command outputs and action type distribution features.\n101\tThese features are fed to three classifiers: Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).\n102\tBecause several structural features overlap with the signals used by the labeling functions (e.g., repetition ratios relate to $\\lambda_1$), structural classifiers can partially reconstruct the LF voting process.\n103\tWe discuss this circularity and its implications in \\S\\ref{sec:main_results}.\n104\t\n105\t\\subsubsection{NL Embedding Probe (ProgressProbe)}\n106\t\\label{sec:progressprobe}\n107\t\n108\tEach action $a_t$ is encoded as a natural language description and embedded using Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \\citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} into a 1024-dimensional vector.\n109\tThe $k$ embeddings within a window form a sequence that is processed by a 2-layer GRU \\citep{cho2014gru} with hidden size 256 and dropout 0.1.\n110\tAn attention mechanism \\citep{bahdanau2015attention} computes a weighted combination of the GRU hidden states, yielding a single window-level representation.\n111\tThis representation is passed through a fully connected layer with sigmoid activation for binary classification.\n112\tTraining uses binary cross-entropy loss with early stopping (patience 5) on a validation set.\n113\t\n114\tThe GRU architecture is motivated by the sequential nature of actions within a window: the order in which commands are issued (e.g., repeated failures followed by a different approach) carries diagnostic information that bag-of-embeddings methods discard.\n115\tWe also evaluate two simpler embedding baselines to isolate the contribution of temporal modeling: (1)~mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression, and (2)~cosine similarity between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.\n116\t\n117\t\\subsubsection{LLM-as-Judge Baseline}\n118\t\\label{sec:llm_judge}\n119\t\n120\tWe prompt Claude Sonnet 4.6 in zero-shot mode to classify each window as stagnation or progress, given the textual descriptions of actions within the window.\n121\tThis baseline serves exclusively for evaluation---it is not used in the silver label pipeline and incurs \\$11.66 in API cost for the SWE-Agent test set alone, making it impractical for large-scale labeling.\n122\t\n123\t%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n124\t\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\n125\t\\label{sec:temporal_models}\n126\t\n127\tThe three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches relative to stagnation onset (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}): $\\lambda_1$ leads by ${\\sim}2$ steps, $\\lambda_2$ is near-concurrent, and $\\lambda_3$ lags by ${\\sim}7$ steps.\n128\tThis motivates exploring whether encoding temporal structure into label aggregation can improve over majority vote.\n129\t\n130\t\\subsubsection{Time-Varying Weights (TWV)}\n131\t\\label{sec:twv}\n132\t\n133\tTWV applies Gaussian kernel smoothing to each LF's activation sequence:\n134\t\\begin{equation}\n135\t\\tilde{\\lambda}_j(t) = \\sum_{t'} K_\\sigma(t - t') \\cdot \\lambda_j(t')\n136\t\\label{eq:twv}\n137\t\\end{equation}\n138\twhere $K_\\sigma$ is a Gaussian kernel with bandwidth $\\sigma$.\n139\tSmoothed activations replace raw votes for conf${=}1$ windows (where exactly one LF disagrees), potentially flipping labels in temporally ambiguous regions.\n140\tThe bandwidth $\\sigma$ controls the extent of temporal context: larger values incorporate more distant steps.\n141\t\n142\t\\subsubsection{Hidden Markov Models}\n143\t\\label{sec:hmm}\n144\t\n145\tWe model the label sequence as a Hidden Markov Model \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm} with two hidden states (stagnation, progress) and observations defined by the joint activation pattern of the three LFs.\n146\tTransition and emission parameters are estimated via Expectation-Maximization (EM).\n147\tThe HMM captures temporal persistence---stagnation episodes tend to span multiple consecutive windows---which majority vote, operating independently per window, cannot model.\n148\t\n149\t\\subsubsection{Lag-Aware HMM}\n150\t\\label{sec:lag_hmm}\n151\t\n152\tThe standard HMM treats all LFs as synchronous.\n153\tLag-Aware HMM extends this by explicitly modeling each LF's temporal offset: $\\lambda_1$ is shifted by $-2$ steps, $\\lambda_2$ by $-1$, and $\\lambda_3$ by $+7$, estimated from the temporal dynamics analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:lf_temporal}).\n154\tTo prevent divergence, we apply two regularization strategies: (1)~\\emph{confidence anchoring}, which fixes labels for conf${\\geq}$2 windows and restricts EM updates to conf${=}1$ windows; and (2)~a prior strength of 5000 on transition parameters.\n155\tThese constraints keep the model close to the majority vote baseline while allowing it to resolve temporally ambiguous labels.\n156\tThe intuition is that aligning each LF's activation to its natural temporal role should produce cleaner emission estimates and, consequently, more accurate Viterbi decodings on ambiguous windows."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"fe80429f-6cb9-424b-91ae-2efff299263e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:35:41.653Z"}
