{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VAdCkHeZCwLpGjdM1aLkmJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n3\t\n4\tWe presented a systematic study of progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents, comparing structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge approaches.\n5\tThree findings emerge.\n6\t\n7\tFirst, structural features dominate zero-shot cross-agent transfer.\n8\tThirty lightweight features achieve 0.995 in-domain and 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1, outperforming neural probes by 36.9\\,pp and zero-shot LLM judges by 30.4\\,pp.\n9\tWith full fine-tuning the probe reaches within 1\\,pp of the structural baseline (0.976 vs.\\ 0.967), but structural features remain the default for zero-data deployment.\n10\tThese features are also data-efficient, maintaining F1 above 0.987 with only 10\\% of training data.\n11\t\n12\tSecond, labeling functions exhibit agent-specific temporal dynamics.\n13\tCommand repetition leads stagnation onset by 1.87 steps on SWE-Agent but lags by 3.32 steps on OpenHands.\n14\tA label quality audit reveals cmd\\_rep is a high-precision detector (97.91\\% precision) whose core value is coverage: prevalence-matched ablation shows removing it collapses training data 10$\\times$ (43K$\\to$4K) and costs $-$29.4\\,pp (p$\\approx$0).\n15\tLabel version bias---not LF noise---was the dominant cross-agent transfer bottleneck.\n16\t\n17\tThird, no label model improves over majority vote.\n18\tFive label models---three temporal (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) and two generative (FlyingSquid, Snorkel MeTaL)---all fail to surpass majority vote; Snorkel MeTaL collapses catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp).\n19\tThe bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation method: three binary LFs produce insufficient inter-LF conflict for any approach to exploit.\n20\t\n21\tAgent monitoring should therefore prioritize lightweight structural features ({<}5\\,ms per window, no GPU) over embedding or LLM approaches for zero-data deployment; when labeled target-agent data is available, neural probes become competitive.\n22\tThe circular evaluation caveat applies to in-domain results; a feature overlap ablation shows that removing all LF-overlapping features retains 0.952 in-domain but collapses cross-agent to 0.566, confirming that the generalizing features encode universal stagnation signals.\n23\t\n24\tFuture work includes extending evaluation to non-software-engineering agent domains, online integration with early termination or strategy switching, scaling to {>}10 labeling functions to test whether temporal aggregation becomes useful, and validation against the pending 650-window gold set."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"fa10202c-6bd5-4574-a133-30fc63f71ccb","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:35:51.953Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CMLmh2LMZ1eGngY2NAGLPS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RnCeHxRqd4NdGe6TMTuLHu","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/abstract.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5903,"cache_read_input_tokens":33997,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5903,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"71567d42-d887-4624-9df9-d8c692aecd29"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018CNBSXLmWtx15ZPzqKLKft","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YNVWQpffkWoC5ACZ6LfVQ4","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Do NOT consult any external information — review only what is presented below.\n\n---\n\n## Paper: \"Circular Evaluation as Systematic Risk in Weak Supervision for Agent Progress Monitoring\"\n\n**Abstract:** Weak supervision pipelines for detecting progress stagnation in LLM agents face a subtle but critical evaluation pitfall: when both training labels and evaluation metrics derive from the same labeling functions (LFs), methods that reverse-engineer LF heuristics appear dominant while methods that learn genuine semantic signals are penalized. The authors construct a 650-window human-annotated benchmark (κ=0.76) from SWE-agent trajectories and reveal that ~22% of majority-vote labels are incorrect—predominantly missing semantically diverse stagnation where agents perform varied but ineffective actions. Structural feature classifiers, which achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels, collapse to 0.78–0.80 on gold labels (−19.5 to −21.5pp), while embedding-based probes drop only 8.6–10.1pp and surpass structural methods on gold (0.82–0.84 vs. 0.78–0.80). An overlap ablation isolating 12 LF-correlated features confirms that >55% of structural signal is circular (residual F1=0.70). Instance-isolated cross-agent evaluation reduces structural GB from 0.967 to 0.89 (−7.7pp), shifting the probe crossover point from 50% to ~8% fine-tuning data. LLM-as-judge with 5-shot chain-of-thought reaches 0.81 gold F1, matching probe and structural baselines. Results reframe the narrative from \"structural dominance\" to \"circular evaluation as systematic risk,\" contributing an annotated benchmark, a quantification methodology for circular bias, and evidence that embeddings learn beyond LF heuristics.\n\n**Section 1 – Introduction:** Introduces agent progress stagnation as a key operational challenge. Weak supervision with LFs (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation) and majority vote. Prior work showed structural classifiers achieving >0.99 F1. Paper argues this \"dominance\" is circular evaluation artifact. 5 key findings plus 3 contributions: (1) 650-window benchmark, (2) circular evaluation quantification methodology, (3) evidence embeddings learn beyond LFs.\n\n**Section 2 – Related Work:**\n- Agent Progress Monitoring: Cycle detectors CDCS+CDSA and SpecRA capture repetitive loops but miss semantically diverse stagnation. SlopCodeBench documents structural erosion in 77% and rising verbosity in 75.5% of long trajectories. Process Reward Models (PRMs) via TD/GAE but require outcome labels and RL infrastructure.\n- Weak Supervision for Sequential Data: Data programming and extensions assume conditionally independent LFs. In sequential settings, LFs exhibit temporal dynamics violating i.i.d. assumption. Paper exposes circular evaluation as further challenge.\n- LLM Self-Evaluation: Reflexion, LATS depend on stuck model's own judgment. External LLM-as-judge is independent but costly.\n\n**Section 3 – Problem Setup:** Trajectories → k=5 overlapping windows. Binary classification: progress vs. stagnation. 3 LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation. MV with conf≥2. Methods: Structural RF/GB (30 features), Embedding probes (GRU over Qwen3/E5/BGE), LLM-as-judge (Claude Sonnet 4), Temporal label models (negative finding).\n\n**Section 4 – Gold Benchmark:** N=650, stratified, two annotators + adjudication, κ=0.76. 22% MV label error. Disagreement analysis: 60.1% MV=prog→gold=stag, 39.9% MV=stag→gold=prog.\n\n**Section 5 – Experiments:**\n- 5.1 Gold-Validated Method Comparison: Structural collapses on gold (−19.5 to −21.5pp), probes gold-robust (−8.6 to −10.1pp), probes surpass structural on gold. All methods converge to 0.78–0.84.\n- 5.2 LLM-as-Judge Few-Shot CoT: 0.81 gold F1, rank inversion vs. LF labels.\n- 5.3 Overlap Ablation: 12 overlap features removed → gold F1 drops 0.80→0.70. >55% circular signal. Residual F1=0.70 still above random but 12-14pp below probes.\n- 5.4 Instance-Isolated Cross-Agent: 847 shared instances (28.3%). Structural GB drops 0.967→0.89. Probe crossover shifts from 50% to ~8% FT.\n\n**Section 6 – Discussion:** Three-pronged evidence chain. Probe gold-robustness. Method convergence at annotation ceiling. Practical deployment recommendations.\n\n**Section 7 – Conclusion + Limitations:** Single agent for gold set; two agents for cross-agent; κ=0.76 limits ceiling; temporal label model negative finding limited to 3-LF regime.\n\n**Notable:** Several references in the paper appear as \"?\" or \"??\" — broken citations.\n\n---\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review with these sections:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points, focus on novelty, framing, positioning, related work completeness)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points, focus on novelty, framing, positioning, related work completeness)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n### 5. Novelty Assessment (is the contribution sufficiently novel for a top venue?)\n### 6. Related Work Gaps (any missing references or connections?)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n### 8. Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Assess whether the claimed novelty is genuine, whether the paper is well-positioned against prior work, and whether the contributions meet the bar of a top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate). Note any presentation issues that undermine the paper's credibility."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29888,"cache_read_input_tokens":23498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29888},"output_tokens":24,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"3a53c6e7-2adb-4df6-9a2a-5fe1abff291f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ae5b4b50404855b8e","tool_use_id":"toolu_01YNVWQpffkWoC5ACZ6LfVQ4","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Do NOT consult any external information — review only what is presented below.\n\n---\n\n## Paper: \"Circular Evaluation as Systematic Risk in Weak Supervision for Agent Progress Monitoring\"\n\n**Abstract:** Weak supervision pipelines for detecting progress stagnation in LLM agents face a subtle but critical evaluation pitfall: when both training labels and evaluation metrics derive from the same labeling functions (LFs), methods that reverse-engineer LF heuristics appear dominant while methods that learn genuine semantic signals are penalized. The authors construct a 650-window human-annotated benchmark (κ=0.76) from SWE-agent trajectories and reveal that ~22% of majority-vote labels are incorrect—predominantly missing semantically diverse stagnation where agents perform varied but ineffective actions. Structural feature classifiers, which achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels, collapse to 0.78–0.80 on gold labels (−19.5 to −21.5pp), while embedding-based probes drop only 8.6–10.1pp and surpass structural methods on gold (0.82–0.84 vs. 0.78–0.80). An overlap ablation isolating 12 LF-correlated features confirms that >55% of structural signal is circular (residual F1=0.70). Instance-isolated cross-agent evaluation reduces structural GB from 0.967 to 0.89 (−7.7pp), shifting the probe crossover point from 50% to ~8% fine-tuning data. LLM-as-judge with 5-shot chain-of-thought reaches 0.81 gold F1, matching probe and structural baselines. Results reframe the narrative from \"structural dominance\" to \"circular evaluation as systematic risk,\" contributing an annotated benchmark, a quantification methodology for circular bias, and evidence that embeddings learn beyond LF heuristics.\n\n**Section 1 – Introduction:** Introduces agent progress stagnation as a key operational challenge. Weak supervision with LFs (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation) and majority vote. Prior work showed structural classifiers achieving >0.99 F1. Paper argues this \"dominance\" is circular evaluation artifact. 5 key findings plus 3 contributions: (1) 650-window benchmark, (2) circular evaluation quantification methodology, (3) evidence embeddings learn beyond LFs.\n\n**Section 2 – Related Work:**\n- Agent Progress Monitoring: Cycle detectors CDCS+CDSA and SpecRA capture repetitive loops but miss semantically diverse stagnation. SlopCodeBench documents structural erosion in 77% and rising verbosity in 75.5% of long trajectories. Process Reward Models (PRMs) via TD/GAE but require outcome labels and RL infrastructure.\n- Weak Supervision for Sequential Data: Data programming and extensions assume conditionally independent LFs. In sequential settings, LFs exhibit temporal dynamics violating i.i.d. assumption. Paper exposes circular evaluation as further challenge.\n- LLM Self-Evaluation: Reflexion, LATS depend on stuck model's own judgment. External LLM-as-judge is independent but costly.\n\n**Section 3 – Problem Setup:** Trajectories → k=5 overlapping windows. Binary classification: progress vs. stagnation. 3 LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation. MV with conf≥2. Methods: Structural RF/GB (30 features), Embedding probes (GRU over Qwen3/E5/BGE), LLM-as-judge (Claude Sonnet 4), Temporal label models (negative finding).\n\n**Section 4 – Gold Benchmark:** N=650, stratified, two annotators + adjudication, κ=0.76. 22% MV label error. Disagreement analysis: 60.1% MV=prog→gold=stag, 39.9% MV=stag→gold=prog.\n\n**Section 5 – Experiments:**\n- 5.1 Gold-Validated Method Comparison: Structural collapses on gold (−19.5 to −21.5pp), probes gold-robust (−8.6 to −10.1pp), probes surpass structural on gold. All methods converge to 0.78–0.84.\n- 5.2 LLM-as-Judge Few-Shot CoT: 0.81 gold F1, rank inversion vs. LF labels.\n- 5.3 Overlap Ablation: 12 overlap features removed → gold F1 drops 0.80→0.70. >55% circular signal. Residual F1=0.70 still above random but 12-14pp below probes.\n- 5.4 Instance-Isolated Cross-Agent: 847 shared instances (28.3%). Structural GB drops 0.967→0.89. Probe crossover shifts from 50% to ~8% FT.\n\n**Section 6 – Discussion:** Three-pronged evidence chain. Probe gold-robustness. Method convergence at annotation ceiling. Practical deployment recommendations.\n\n**Section 7 – Conclusion + Limitations:** Single agent for gold set; two agents for cross-agent; κ=0.76 limits ceiling; temporal label model negative finding limited to 3-LF regime.\n\n**Notable:** Several references in the paper appear as \"?\" or \"??\" — broken citations.\n\n---\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review with these sections:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points, focus on novelty, framing, positioning, related work completeness)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points, focus on novelty, framing, positioning, related work completeness)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n### 5. Novelty Assessment (is the contribution sufficiently novel for a top venue?)\n### 6. Related Work Gaps (any missing references or connections?)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n### 8. Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Assess whether the claimed novelty is genuine, whether the paper is well-positioned against prior work, and whether the contributions meet the bar of a top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate). Note any presentation issues that undermine the paper's credibility.","uuid":"bc5ff162-4df2-45e4-bdc1-0989fc3a13d7","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\nReview the following paper and produce a structured review. Do NOT consult any external information — review only what is presented below.\n\n---\n\n## Paper: \"Circular Evaluation as Systematic Risk in Weak Supervision for Agent Progress Monitoring\"\n\n**Abstract:** Weak supervision pipelines for detecting progress stagnation in LLM agents face a subtle but critical evaluation pitfall: when both training labels and evaluation metrics derive from the same labeling functions (LFs), methods that reverse-engineer LF heuristics appear dominant while methods that learn genuine semantic signals are penalized. The authors construct a 650-window human-annotated benchmark (κ=0.76) from SWE-agent trajectories and reveal that ~22% of majority-vote labels are incorrect—predominantly missing semantically diverse stagnation where agents perform varied but ineffective actions. Structural feature classifiers, which achieve near-perfect F1 (0.995) on LF-derived labels, collapse to 0.78–0.80 on gold labels (−19.5 to −21.5pp), while embedding-based probes drop only 8.6–10.1pp and surpass structural methods on gold (0.82–0.84 vs. 0.78–0.80). An overlap ablation isolating 12 LF-correlated features confirms that >55% of structural signal is circular (residual F1=0.70). Instance-isolated cross-agent evaluation reduces structural GB from 0.967 to 0.89 (−7.7pp), shifting the probe crossover point from 50% to ~8% fine-tuning data. LLM-as-judge with 5-shot chain-of-thought reaches 0.81 gold F1, matching probe and structural baselines. Results reframe the narrative from \"structural dominance\" to \"circular evaluation as systematic risk,\" contributing an annotated benchmark, a quantification methodology for circular bias, and evidence that embeddings learn beyond LF heuristics.\n\n**Section 1 – Introduction:** Introduces agent progress stagnation as a key operational challenge. Weak supervision with LFs (cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation) and majority vote. Prior work showed structural classifiers achieving >0.99 F1. Paper argues this \"dominance\" is circular evaluation artifact. 5 key findings plus 3 contributions: (1) 650-window benchmark, (2) circular evaluation quantification methodology, (3) evidence embeddings learn beyond LFs.\n\n**Section 2 – Related Work:**\n- Agent Progress Monitoring: Cycle detectors CDCS+CDSA and SpecRA capture repetitive loops but miss semantically diverse stagnation. SlopCodeBench documents structural erosion in 77% and rising verbosity in 75.5% of long trajectories. Process Reward Models (PRMs) via TD/GAE but require outcome labels and RL infrastructure.\n- Weak Supervision for Sequential Data: Data programming and extensions assume conditionally independent LFs. In sequential settings, LFs exhibit temporal dynamics violating i.i.d. assumption. Paper exposes circular evaluation as further challenge.\n- LLM Self-Evaluation: Reflexion, LATS depend on stuck model's own judgment. External LLM-as-judge is independent but costly.\n\n**Section 3 – Problem Setup:** Trajectories → k=5 overlapping windows. Binary classification: progress vs. stagnation. 3 LFs: cmd_rep, file_diff, outcome_backpropagation. MV with conf≥2. Methods: Structural RF/GB (30 features), Embedding probes (GRU over Qwen3/E5/BGE), LLM-as-judge (Claude Sonnet 4), Temporal label models (negative finding).\n\n**Section 4 – Gold Benchmark:** N=650, stratified, two annotators + adjudication, κ=0.76. 22% MV label error. Disagreement analysis: 60.1% MV=prog→gold=stag, 39.9% MV=stag→gold=prog.\n\n**Section 5 – Experiments:**\n- 5.1 Gold-Validated Method Comparison: Structural collapses on gold (−19.5 to −21.5pp), probes gold-robust (−8.6 to −10.1pp), probes surpass structural on gold. All methods converge to 0.78–0.84.\n- 5.2 LLM-as-Judge Few-Shot CoT: 0.81 gold F1, rank inversion vs. LF labels.\n- 5.3 Overlap Ablation: 12 overlap features removed → gold F1 drops 0.80→0.70. >55% circular signal. Residual F1=0.70 still above random but 12-14pp below probes.\n- 5.4 Instance-Isolated Cross-Agent: 847 shared instances (28.3%). Structural GB drops 0.967→0.89. Probe crossover shifts from 50% to ~8% FT.\n\n**Section 6 – Discussion:** Three-pronged evidence chain. Probe gold-robustness. Method convergence at annotation ceiling. Practical deployment recommendations.\n\n**Section 7 – Conclusion + Limitations:** Single agent for gold set; two agents for cross-agent; κ=0.76 limits ceiling; temporal label model negative finding limited to 3-LF regime.\n\n**Notable:** Several references in the paper appear as \"?\" or \"??\" — broken citations.\n\n---\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review with these sections:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bullet points, focus on novelty, framing, positioning, related work completeness)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bullet points, focus on novelty, framing, positioning, related work completeness)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n### 5. Novelty Assessment (is the contribution sufficiently novel for a top venue?)\n### 6. Related Work Gaps (any missing references or connections?)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n### 8. Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous. Assess whether the claimed novelty is genuine, whether the paper is well-positioned against prior work, and whether the contributions meet the bar of a top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate). Note any presentation issues that undermine the paper's credibility."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YNVWQpffkWoC5ACZ6LfVQ4","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"7dc95931-c826-4065-a5ec-e75f37700665","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:35:52.963Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RnCeHxRqd4NdGe6TMTuLHu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Abstract: Structural stagnation detection in LLM coding agents (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n2\tLLM-based agents deployed on software engineering tasks frequently exhibit semantic stagnation---executing diverse yet collectively unproductive action sequences that evade repetition detectors and overwhelm LLM-based judges. No systematic study has investigated what signals carry stagnation information or whether detection requires semantic understanding.\n3\t\n4\tWe address this gap with a weak supervision pipeline (3 labeling functions + majority vote) over 205K+ windows from two architecturally distinct SE agents, SWE-Agent and OpenHands, comparing 30 structural features, a GRU-based neural probe, and an LLM-as-judge.\n5\t\n6\tOur central finding is that structural features dominate zero-shot cross-agent transfer: 30 features achieve RF 0.995 in-domain and GB 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1, outperforming the neural probe zero-shot by 36.9\\,pp and the zero-shot LLM-as-judge by 30.4\\,pp. With full fine-tuning the probe reaches within 1\\,pp of the structural baseline (0.976 vs.\\ 0.967), but structural features remain the default for zero-data deployment. They are also data-efficient, exceeding 0.987 F1 at 10\\% training data. In-domain evaluation reflects circular agreement with LF-derived silver labels; cross-agent transfer and a pending gold set provide non-circular validation.\n7\t\n8\tLabeling functions exhibit agent-specific temporal dynamics: command repetition leads stagnation by 1.87 steps on SWE-Agent but lags by 3.32 steps on OpenHands. A label quality audit reveals cmd\\_rep is a high-precision detector (97.91\\%) whose value is coverage; removing it collapses training data 10$\\times$ and costs $-$29.4\\,pp. Label version bias---not LF noise---was the dominant cross-agent transfer bottleneck.\n9\t\n10\tDespite these well-characterized temporal patterns, five label models---three temporal (time-varying weights, HMM, lag-aware HMM) and two generative (FlyingSquid, Snorkel MeTaL)---fail to improve over majority vote, with Snorkel MeTaL collapsing catastrophically ($-$63.6\\,pp). The bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation method: the 3-function regime produces insufficient inter-LF conflict for any approach to exploit.\n11\t\n12\t\\goldset{Gold set validation (650 windows) pending.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTzEvuF3MgnKCiVzABa5ew","session_id":"d03bea4a-7b88-4764-ab68-ded012e337db","uuid":"1f8229ed-502a-4b05-8a18-41ddc1a89c8f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:35:52.429Z"}
